1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,784
It's not, and it's worth being precise about why, because "it searches a vector

2
00:00:04,784 --> 00:00:06,917
database" isn't an explanation either.

3
00:00:06,917 --> 00:00:10,340
It's a phrase people repeat to sound like they understand it.

4
00:00:10,340 --> 00:00:14,662
An embedding is a list of numbers that represents what a piece of text means.

5
00:00:14,662 --> 00:00:17,019
Not what words it contains, what it means.

6
00:00:17,019 --> 00:00:21,621
Two sentences that say roughly the same thing in completely different words end up

7
00:00:21,621 --> 00:00:22,800
with similar numbers.

8
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:26,392
Two sentences using identical words in unrelated contexts don't.

9
00:00:26,392 --> 00:00:30,994
Vector search is just this: turn your question into that same kind of number list,

10
00:00:30,994 --> 00:00:34,642
then find whichever stored piece of text has the closest numbers.

11
00:00:34,642 --> 00:00:36,775
Closest numbers means closest meaning.

12
00:00:36,775 --> 00:00:41,321
That's the entire mechanism, and you now understand it as well as you need to for

13
00:00:41,321 --> 00:00:43,566
every conversation you'll have about it.

14
00:00:43,566 --> 00:00:48,112
What actually changes how you use this is one consequence: the search is entirely

15
00:00:48,112 --> 00:00:50,693
about meaning, never the specific words typed.

16
00:00:50,693 --> 00:00:55,239
Ask about "cancelling a subscription" and it can surface "ending your membership"

17
00:00:55,239 --> 00:00:57,821
without either sentence sharing a single word.

18
00:00:57,821 --> 00:01:01,245
That's the whole point of replacing keyword search with this.

19
00:01:01,795 --> 00:01:06,056
You'll never build this pipeline yourself, so treat what follows as the machinery

20
00:01:06,056 --> 00:01:10,159
behind one decision that actually is yours: who owns re-indexing when a source

21
00:01:10,159 --> 00:01:14,525
document changes, which the honest caveat at the end of this lecture comes back to.

22
00:01:14,525 --> 00:01:18,523
Walk the two paths, because they run at completely different times, and that

23
00:01:18,523 --> 00:01:22,363
distinction explains a lot of confusion about what's slow and what isn't.

24
00:01:22,363 --> 00:01:25,151
The top path runs once, when you load your documents.

25
00:01:25,151 --> 00:01:29,412
Each document gets split into chunks, sized to be small enough to be specific and

26
00:01:29,412 --> 00:01:31,832
large enough to still make sense on their own.

27
00:01:31,832 --> 00:01:36,145
Each chunk becomes a number list, and all of those number lists get stored in what

28
00:01:36,145 --> 00:01:40,406
people call a vector index, which is really just a very fast lookup structure for

29
00:01:40,406 --> 00:01:44,825
"which numbers are near which other numbers." The bottom path runs every single time

30
00:01:44,825 --> 00:01:46,087
someone asks a question.

31
00:01:46,087 --> 00:01:49,822
The question becomes its own number list, using the exact same process.

32
00:01:49,822 --> 00:01:53,925
The system finds whichever stored chunks have the closest numbers, the nearest

33
00:01:53,925 --> 00:01:58,186
neighbours, and those chunks, not the whole document, get dropped into the prompt

34
00:01:58,186 --> 00:01:59,396
alongside the question.

35
00:01:59,396 --> 00:02:00,816
That's the whole mechanism.

36
00:02:00,816 --> 00:02:04,867
Everything people call RAG is some version of these six steps, with different

37
00:02:04,867 --> 00:02:09,233
choices about chunk size, how many neighbours to retrieve, and what counts as close

38
00:02:09,233 --> 00:02:09,601
enough.

39
00:02:10,151 --> 00:02:14,684
Here's six chunks, ranked by how close their numbers are to the question's numbers,

40
00:02:14,684 --> 00:02:15,721
for one real query.

41
00:02:15,721 --> 00:02:18,124
Notice the system doesn't return one answer.

42
00:02:18,124 --> 00:02:22,657
It returns a shortlist, usually somewhere between three and ten chunks, and someone

43
00:02:22,657 --> 00:02:23,640
chose that number.

44
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:27,189
Set it too low and you miss a chunk that actually had the answer.

45
00:02:27,189 --> 00:02:31,449
Set it too high and you flood the prompt with marginally related material that

46
00:02:31,449 --> 00:02:35,271
crowds out the good chunks, and costs you tokens on every single call.

47
00:02:35,271 --> 00:02:39,149
That cutoff line is doing more work than most teams give it credit for.

48
00:02:39,149 --> 00:02:41,988
It's not a technical detail buried in a config file.

49
00:02:41,988 --> 00:02:46,138
It's a product decision about how much you trust the ranking, and it's worth

50
00:02:46,138 --> 00:02:50,616
revisiting deliberately instead of leaving at whatever default a tutorial happened

51
00:02:50,616 --> 00:02:50,999
to use.

52
00:02:51,549 --> 00:02:55,722
This is the failure mode worth knowing by name, because it looks like a model

53
00:02:55,722 --> 00:02:58,162
problem and it's actually a chunking problem.

54
00:02:58,162 --> 00:03:02,390
Split a document every fixed number of characters, and you'll eventually cut a

55
00:03:02,390 --> 00:03:06,780
numbered list in half, or separate a table from the header row that explains what

56
00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:07,702
its columns mean.

57
00:03:07,702 --> 00:03:11,063
The embedding for that fragment is still technically accurate.

58
00:03:11,063 --> 00:03:13,664
It represents what little text survived the cut.

59
00:03:13,664 --> 00:03:18,055
It's just useless to whoever reads it, because half a procedure with no header is

60
00:03:18,055 --> 00:03:18,922
not a procedure.

61
00:03:18,922 --> 00:03:22,934
Chunk by the document's actual structure, sections, list boundaries, table

62
00:03:22,934 --> 00:03:27,216
boundaries, and the same retrieval step returns something a human could act on.

63
00:03:27,216 --> 00:03:28,950
Nothing about the model changed.

64
00:03:28,950 --> 00:03:33,124
The only thing that changed was where the document got cut before it was ever

65
00:03:33,124 --> 00:03:33,612
embedded.

66
00:03:34,162 --> 00:03:38,481
Say the honest caveat, because "we added RAG" gets treated as a guarantee of

67
00:03:38,481 --> 00:03:40,015
accuracy, and it isn't one.

68
00:03:40,015 --> 00:03:42,970
An embedding captures what a piece of text is about.

69
00:03:42,970 --> 00:03:45,982
It has no opinion on whether that text is still true.

70
00:03:45,982 --> 00:03:50,699
A pricing page from eight months ago and this week's pricing page can both describe

71
00:03:50,699 --> 00:03:55,018
pricing in nearly identical language, and rank almost identically close to a

72
00:03:55,018 --> 00:03:56,211
question about price.

73
00:03:56,211 --> 00:04:00,757
Retrieval will happily hand over the old one if it's still sitting in the index.

74
00:04:00,757 --> 00:04:02,519
That's not a bug in the search.

75
00:04:02,519 --> 00:04:06,951
It's exactly what similarity means, and it's why keeping your source documents

76
00:04:06,951 --> 00:04:11,611
current is part of running a RAG feature, not a one-time setup step you finish and

77
00:04:11,611 --> 00:04:12,009
forget.

78
00:04:12,009 --> 00:04:16,669
On any RAG feature you touch: who owns re-indexing when a source document changes?

79
00:04:16,669 --> 00:04:21,044
If the honest answer is nobody, you've found next week's actual risk, sitting

80
00:04:21,044 --> 00:04:24,454
quietly underneath a feature everyone already signed off on.

81
00:04:25,004 --> 00:04:29,116
That's the definition worth keeping, and notice what it deliberately leaves out.

82
00:04:29,116 --> 00:04:33,176
No mention of correctness, because that was never what this mechanism promises.

83
00:04:33,176 --> 00:04:34,770
The exercise makes it concrete.

84
00:04:34,770 --> 00:04:38,984
Find one real query your feature has handled, look at what actually got retrieved,

85
00:04:38,984 --> 00:04:40,680
and read it the way a user would.

86
00:04:40,680 --> 00:04:43,405
If it's not useful, the fix is rarely a better model.

87
00:04:43,405 --> 00:04:47,671
It's almost always upstream, in how the document got chunked or how many neighbours

88
00:04:47,671 --> 00:04:48,236
got pulled.

89
00:04:48,786 --> 00:04:50,305
One thing before you move on.

90
00:04:50,305 --> 00:04:54,653
Pull one real retrieved result from your own feature and read it properly, not just

91
00:04:54,653 --> 00:04:55,072
skim it.

92
00:04:55,072 --> 00:04:59,368
Then check how the source document got chunked, and whether the boundaries respect

93
00:04:59,368 --> 00:05:02,930
lists, tables, and sections, or just cut at a fixed character count.

94
00:05:02,930 --> 00:05:06,964
Next lecture stays in the stack and tackles the phrase that swallowed "prompt

95
00:05:06,964 --> 00:05:11,364
engineering" whole: what context engineering actually means, and why the name change

96
00:05:11,364 --> 00:05:11,783
matters.
